Random Forest simulation instructions

Please create a simulation as follows:

General parameters: 
- Number of samples for training is called ‘ntrain’ and is equal to 4000
- The number of samples for testing is called ‘ntest’ and is equal to 2000 
- The number of clusters in the training dataset is termed as ‘k’: we will do simulations from ‘k’ = 2, 4, 8, 12, 16
- The number of covariates to simulate is termed as ’S’: for all simulations, S = 20: these covariates are named ‘X1’, ‘X2’, etc all the way up to ‘X20’
- To simulate the true outcome model:
    - draw ’S’ coefficients from a standard normal distribution - these will be coefficients for the linear part of function: call this vector of coefficients ‘linear_coefs’
    - Draw 5 more coefficients also from a standard normal distribution - these will be coefficients for the nonlinear part of the function: call this vector ‘nonlinear_coefs’ 
    - Create a outcome simulation function that does the following for a given dataset containing covariate data: 
        - Use the ‘linear_coefs’ vector to simulate the linear part of the outcome, by doing matrix multiplication with covariate_data as follows: covariate_data @ linear_coefs. Then, use the ‘nonlinear_coefs’ as follows: nonlinear_coefs[0]*sin(X1) + nonlinear_coefs[1]*sin(X2) + nonlinear_coefs[2]*X3*X4 + nonlinear_coefs[3]*X3^2 + nonlinear_coefs[4]*X4^3
        - Add standard normal noise to each outcome


For the ‘Ensemble’ method:
- Simulate ‘ntrain’ samples from an S-dimensional Uniform[0, 1/k] distribution so that the train dataset is dimension ntrain x S: each of these these variables are named ‘X1’ up to ‘X20’ since S = 20.
- Simulate ‘ntest’ samples from an S-dimensional Uniform[0, 1/k] distribution so that the test dataset is dimension ntest x S: same variable names as training dataset
- Simulate outcome values for the train and test dataset by using the outcome simulation function created above
- Train a Random Forest with 250 trees on the train dataset + outcome
- Test on the test covariate data, and then compute the MSE comparing the predictions to the true test outcome

For the ‘Merged’ method:
- For b = 1,…,k: simulate ‘ntrain/k’ samples from an S-dimensional Uniform[(b-1)/k, b/k] distribution so that the train dataset is dimension ntrain x S: each of these these variables are named ‘X1’ up to ‘X20’ since S = 20.
- Simulate ‘ntest’ samples from an S-dimensional Uniform[0, 1/k] distribution so that the test dataset is dimension ntest x S: same variable names as training dataset
- Simulate outcome values for the train and test dataset by using the outcome simulation function created above
- Train a Random Forest with 250 trees on the train dataset + outcome
- Test on the test covariate data, and then compute the MSE comparing the predictions to the true test outcome

Now, take the ratio of the ‘Ensemble’ MSE to the ‘Merged’ MSE. This is the output value for one replicate. 


Complete the above simulation for ‘nrep’ = 100 replicates for each value of ‘k’, where ‘k’ = 2, 4, 8, 12, 16. For each value of ‘k’, write all of the ratios of ‘Ensemble’ to ‘Merged’ MSE calculated as the output across replicates to a file: by the end, this file should have ’nrep’ rows and ‘k’ columns where each column contains the results for that value of ‘k’. 

